---
import Guide from "../../components/Guide.astro";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What decides who shows up on the Google map?",
    a: "Google names three things: relevance (how well your profile matches the search), distance (how far you are from the searcher or the place they named) and prominence (how well known you are, including reviews and links). You control relevance fully, prominence over time, and distance not at all.",
  },
  {
    q: "Should I hide my address if I work at customers’ homes?",
    a: "Yes, if customers do not visit you. Service-area businesses should hide the address and list the areas they serve instead. Showing a home address you do not serve customers at breaks Google’s guidelines.",
  },
  {
    q: "How many reviews do I need?",
    a: "There is no fixed number. What matters is how you compare with the businesses that show up instead of you, and how recent your reviews are. A steady stream beats a burst.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do Google posts still matter?",
    a: "They are a minor signal, but they keep the profile current and give customers a reason to act. A short post a week is enough.",
  },
];
---

<Guide
  title="Google Business Profile Checklist for Local Businesses (2026) — Azoth & Co."
  description="A field-by-field Google Business Profile checklist, in the order that moves map rankings: categories, services, service area, reviews, photos and posts. Written for owners in Dallas–Fort Worth."
  label="Business Profile"
  heading="The Google Business Profile checklist."
  sub="The free profile that decides whether you show on the map. Every field that matters, in the order it matters."
  published="2026-11-11"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    For most local businesses, the Google Business Profile brings in more calls than the website. It is what shows on
    the map, in the top three results, and increasingly in Google’s AI answer. It is free. It is also, in most of the
    audits we run, half finished.
  </p>
  <p>Work through this list top to bottom. The first section does the most.</p>

  <h2>1. Categories: the biggest single setting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Primary category</strong> is the most specific one that describes your main business. “Medical spa”, not “Beauty salon”. “HVAC contractor”, not “Contractor”.</li>
    <li><strong>Secondary categories</strong> cover the other services you truly offer. Add each one that applies. Skip ones that do not.</li>
    <li>Check what category the top three businesses for your main search use. If they all share one and you do not, that is often why.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Services and products</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>List every service under the matching category, using the words customers search for.</li>
    <li>Add a short description to each one. Say what it is and who it is for.</li>
    <li>Add prices or starting prices where you can. Customers filter on them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Location and service area</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If customers come to you, show the address and set the pin exactly on the door.</li>
    <li>If you go to them, hide the address and list the cities you actually serve.</li>
    <li>Use the same business name, address and phone that appear on your website and listings, character for character.</li>
    <li>Your business name is your real name. No city or keywords added to it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Reviews</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Ask every satisfied customer, the same day, with a direct review link.</li>
    <li>Reply to every review within a few days, good or bad. Mention the service in your reply when it is natural.</li>
    <li>Never buy reviews, gate them, or offer something in exchange for them. Google removes them and the FTC fines for them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. Hours, contact and booking</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Regular hours, plus holiday hours before each holiday.</li>
    <li>A phone number that is answered, and your website link pointing to the most relevant page.</li>
    <li>A booking or appointment link if you take bookings online.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>6. Photos</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Your own photos: the outside, the inside, the team, the work. Stock images do not help.</li>
    <li>Add new ones every month. A profile that changes looks like a business that is open.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>7. Description, Q&amp;A and posts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The description says what you do, for whom and where, in plain sentences. No links or promotions.</li>
    <li>Answer the questions customers ask you on the phone, on the profile and on your site.</li>
    <li>Post an offer, update or recent job about once a week.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>8. The website behind it</h2>
  <p>
    The profile and the site support each other. Every core service needs its own page, the page needs to load fast on a
    phone, and the business name, address and phone on the site should match the profile exactly. If AI answers matter
    to you, read <a href="/visibility/ai-search/">how assistants choose who to recommend</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How to tell if it is working</h2>
  <p>
    The profile’s own performance view shows calls, direction requests, website clicks and the searches that found you.
    Look at it monthly. Then search your main service from a phone in each town you serve and note where you place. That
    is the honest scoreboard.
  </p>
</Guide>
